        "commit_message": "We here avoid diagnostics such as the following without resorting to the\nBazel flag `--incompatible_default_to_explicit_init_py`, which is\ndeprecated as of Bazel 10:\n\n======================================================================\nWARNING: Target @@cython+//:cython_binary is using implicit __init__.py\ncreation.\nThis diabolic behavior is deprecated and will be disabled by default in\na\n      future release.\n      See https://github.com/bazel-contrib/rules_python/issues/2945\n\n      Ensure all __init__.py files are explicitly created and\n      added to the srcs or deps of your targets.\n\n      Disable implicit creation for your module in MODULE.bazel:\n\nrules_python_config =\nuse_extension(\"@rules_python//python/extensions:config.bzl\", \"config\")\n        rules_python_config.explicit_init_py(default = True)\n\n      Or for a specific target by setting:\n\n        legacy_create_init = 0\n\n      Or globally with the following Bazel flag:\n\n        --incompatible_default_to_explicit_init_py\n======================================================================\n\nI saw these diagnostics with rules_python 2.5.0-rc3 and Bazel 9.3.2\n(without the flag `--incompatible_default_to_explicit_init_py`).\n\nNote that `legacy_create_init = 0` is supported in rules_python 2.2.0,\nso there's no need to bump the minimum version of rules_python required\nby cython. The `explicit_init_py()` method first appeared in\nrules_python 2.3.0, so using it to deal with these warnings would\nrequire bumping the minimum version of rules_python. I applied the\n`legacy_create_init = 0` as follows:\n\n    buildozer \"set legacy_create_init 0\" //...:%py_binary\n    buildozer \"set legacy_create_init 0\" //...:%py_test",
        "commit_title": "fix implicit __init__.py creation warnings (#8132)",
